\section{Generate a Tree and Keep the Evidence}

Grammar-derived tests reach structures unlikely to appear when source text is assembled from arbitrary characters. A derivation can also carry an expected tree, source-boundary information, or a record of the productions exercised. \textit{Formal Languages and Compilation}, by Crespi Reghizzi, Breveglieri, and Morzenti, distinguishes concrete grammatical structure from the results of syntax-directed translation. That distinction guides the oracle: a frontend may accept the right tokens while building the wrong tree or associating the wrong source spans with its nodes.

Generate under a depth, node, or token budget. Compute the minimum expansion cost for each productive nonterminal and reserve enough budget to finish the remaining siblings before choosing a recursive alternative. A token budget alone does not bound a derivation if recursive steps can consume no tokens; charge for the expansion steps as well. Track the intended distribution: coverage of every production in isolation does not cover neighboring production pairs, precedence interactions, or lexer boundaries. McIlroy's \textit{Enumerating the Strings of Regular Languages} offers a complementary exhaustive strategy for finite, bounded lexical slices; Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-132} develops it for both accepted and rejected strings.

\section{Worked Test: Preserve the Generated Grouping}

Suppose a language gives multiplication higher precedence than addition. Generate the tree $\mathsf{Mul}(\mathsf{Add}(a,b),c)$, then render it as \texttt{(a+b)*c}. Parsing that text should recover the generated tree after discarding grouping nodes and spans. A pretty-printer that emits \texttt{a+b*c} has produced valid text, but it parses as $\mathsf{Add}(a,\mathsf{Mul}(b,c))$. If the test checks only acceptance, both the formatter bug and some parser bugs go unnoticed.

Use two renderers with different purposes: a simple one that emits explicit parentheses at every binary node, and the candidate formatter that aims to omit unnecessary parentheses. Parse both and compare their normalized trees to the generated original, not just to one another. Make whitespace insertion a separate metamorphic test: inserting spaces at token boundaries declared insignificant must preserve the normalized tree. Exclude boundaries inside multi-character tokens and significant indentation. Check spans separately using known offsets in the rendered text; a normalized equality cannot test spans that it deliberately erases.

For a negative case in a grammar with semicolon-terminated expression statements, remove the closing parenthesis from \texttt{(a+b)*c; z;}, producing \texttt{(a+b*c; z;}. If the specified recovery policy synchronizes at the semicolon, require a diagnostic for the incomplete expression and an intact statement for \texttt{z}. Do not require this property of every random deletion, since many deletions still produce legal text or cause the error to surface at a different token. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-137} discusses the additional obligations for an editor that retains malformed text.

\section{Shrink Failures Under the Same Oracle}

Produce a smaller failing input by replacing a subtree with a smaller node of the same grammar category, reducing a repeated list, or shrinking lexical values. Re-render and rerun the particular assertion after each candidate edit. A shrink that turns the original wrong-tree failure into a lexer rejection has not preserved the failure. Likewise, when minimizing an LR ambiguity witness, preserve both successful derivations rather than just the contested table entry (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-134}). For an edit-sequence test, shrink the sequence as well as the program, but replay from the same initial document so missing history does not create a different problem. Keep the starting seed, the grammar or frontend version, and the minimized source or edit trace; a seed alone may not replay against a changed generator.

Christian Munley, Aaron Jarmusch, and Sunita Chandrasekaran's \textit{LLM4VV: Developing LLM-Driven Testsuite for Compiler Validation} studies model-generated tests for OpenACC, including tests guided by templates. It provides a useful comparison: a generator can propose cases outside a handwritten corpus, but the verdict still needs a separate oracle. In a grammar-based frontend suite, retain exact invariants where they are available, and use independent implementations or explicitly specified metamorphic relations elsewhere. \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/39-semantic-fingerprints-and-metamorphic-compiler-testing.md}{Markdown Dossier Chapter~39} develops those relations at the level of compiler behavior. At the frontend, the key is to decide up front which observable must agree: membership, structure, binding, or diagnostics.
